<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Resetting the accounting basis entirely, in narrow circumstances</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1625 PERMITS an ENTERPRISE, in TWO narrowly DEFINED circumstances, to COMPREHENSIVELY revalue ITS assets and LIABILITIES to FAIR value — ESTABLISHING an ENTIRELY new BASIS of accounting GOING forward. THIS is a GENUINELY distinctive FEATURE within THIS poster LIBRARY'S entire COVERAGE — unmodified IFRS GENERALLY does NOT permit a COMPARABLE "fresh START" revaluation OUTSIDE very NARROW circumstances (Section 7).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Two Narrow Triggering Scenarios</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Purchase transactions and financial reorganizations, only</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Scenario</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Purchase transaction ("push-down accounting")</span><span>WHERE substantially ALL of an ENTERPRISE'S equity INTERESTS are ACQUIRED by ANOTHER party</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Financial reorganization ("fresh start" accounting)</span><span>WHERE an ENTERPRISE emerges FROM a FORMAL financial REORGANIZATION (SUCH as bankruptcy OR restructuring proceedings) MEETING specific CRITERIA</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Scenario One — Push-Down Accounting Following a Purchase Transaction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reflecting the acquirer's own cost basis within the acquired entity's own books</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE SUBSTANTIALLY ALL of an ENTERPRISE'S equity INTERESTS are ACQUIRED (e.g. a PRIVATE company IS purchased IN full BY another PARTY), THE acquired ENTERPRISE may "PUSH DOWN" the ACQUIRER'S own NEW cost BASIS — REVALUING its OWN assets and LIABILITIES to FAIR value WITHIN its OWN separate financial STATEMENTS, RATHER than CONTINUING to REPORT at ITS historical, PRE-acquisition carrying AMOUNTS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Scenario Two — Financial Reorganization ("Fresh Start" Accounting)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A clean accounting slate following formal restructuring</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE an ENTERPRISE emerges FROM a FORMAL financial REORGANIZATION (SUCH as PROCEEDINGS under the COMPANIES' Creditors ARRANGEMENT Act or the BANKRUPTCY and INSOLVENCY Act) MEETING specific CRITERIA — TYPICALLY involving a GENUINE change IN control and a SUBSTANTIAL realignment OF the entity'S capital STRUCTURE — it MAY apply COMPREHENSIVE revaluation, ESTABLISHING a "FRESH start" basis of ACCOUNTING that REFLECTS the entity'S post-REORGANIZATION economic REALITY, RATHER than CARRYING FORWARD its pre-REORGANIZATION historical COST basis.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>What Comprehensive Revaluation Actually Does</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Every asset and liability restated to fair value at once</div><div class="text-supporting">COMPREHENSIVE revaluation REQUIRES restating ALL of an ENTERPRISE'S identifiable ASSETS and LIABILITIES to THEIR fair VALUES as OF the REVALUATION date — NOT a SELECTIVE, item-by-ITEM revaluation, but a COMPLETE, entity-WIDE reset OF the accounting BASIS, MUCH as WOULD occur in a FRESH acquisition ACCOUNTING exercise.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Where the Revaluation Adjustment Is Recognized</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Typically within retained earnings or a separate equity component</div><div class="text-supporting">THE NET adjustment ARISING from the COMPREHENSIVE revaluation is TYPICALLY recognized WITHIN retained EARNINGS (or a SEPARATE component OF equity, DEPENDING on the SPECIFIC circumstances) — ESTABLISHING the NEW, revalued AMOUNTS as the ENTITY'S fresh COST basis GOING forward, RATHER than FLOWING through NET income for the PERIOD.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Why This Is a Genuinely Distinctive Feature</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A rare instance where ASPE goes beyond what IFRS permits</div><div class="text-supporting">UNMODIFIED IFRS GENERALLY does NOT permit PUSH-down accounting OR a COMPARABLE comprehensive "FRESH start" revaluation OUTSIDE of VERY narrow CIRCUMSTANCES — WHILE US GAAP HAS historically PERMITTED a COMPARABLE push-DOWN accounting option UNDER ASC 805-50, WITHIN certain CONDITIONS. ASPE'S own EXPLICIT, dedicated STANDALONE Section for BOTH the push-DOWN and FRESH-start SCENARIOS represents a GENUINELY notable POINT where CANADIAN private-ENTERPRISE GAAP DIVERGES meaningfully FROM the more RESTRICTIVE international IFRS APPROACH, REFLECTING the PARTICULAR relevance OF push-down ACCOUNTING and FRESH-start reporting TO Canadian private-COMPANY acquisition and RESTRUCTURING practice.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Conditions That Must Be Met</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Not an unrestricted, elective option</div><div class="text-supporting">COMPREHENSIVE revaluation is NOT a FREELY elective POLICY choice — it APPLIES ONLY where the SPECIFIC conditions FOR one of the TWO qualifying SCENARIOS (Sections 3-4) are GENUINELY met, ENSURING the OPTION is reserved FOR situations INVOLVING a GENUINE, substantive CHANGE in the ENTERPRISE'S ownership OR capital structure, RATHER than being AVAILABLE for ROUTINE, discretionary REVALUATION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Push-Down Accounting After an Acquisition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Reflecting the new owner's cost basis within the acquired company itself</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A private company acquired in full</div><div class="wk-body">An acquirer purchases 100% of a private target company for $12,000,000. At acquisition, the target's identifiable net assets have a fair value of $10,500,000, previously carried at a historical cost basis of $7,200,000 within the target's own books.</div><div class="wk-answer">The TARGET applies COMPREHENSIVE revaluation, RESTATING its OWN assets and LIABILITIES to THEIR $10,500,000 FAIR value — RECOGNIZING a $10,500,000 − $7,200,000 = <strong>$3,300,000</strong> revaluation ADJUSTMENT, TYPICALLY within RETAINED earnings, ESTABLISHING this NEW basis as the TARGET'S own FRESH accounting STARTING point.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Fresh Start Accounting After a Financial Reorganization</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A clean slate after formal restructuring proceedings</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Emerging from CCAA proceedings</div><div class="wk-body">A private company emerges from formal financial reorganization proceedings, meeting the specific criteria for comprehensive revaluation, with a substantial realignment of its capital structure and a genuine change in control.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY applies COMPREHENSIVE revaluation, RESTATING all IDENTIFIABLE assets and LIABILITIES to FAIR value AS of the REORGANIZATION date — ESTABLISHING a "FRESH start" accounting BASIS that REFLECTS its POST-reorganization economic REALITY, RATHER than CARRYING forward its PRE-reorganization historical COST figures.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1625 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of the event triggering comprehensive revaluation, and confirmation the qualifying conditions were met</span><span>Basis and amount of revaluation adjustments recognized</span><span>Where the revaluation adjustment was recognized within the financial statements</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether the specific conditions for push-down or fresh-start accounting are genuinely met</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of all identifiable assets and liabilities as of the revaluation date</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS and US GAAP Push-Down Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1625 vs IFRS vs US GAAP</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1625</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">Unmodified IFRS</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Push-down accounting</span><span>Permitted, dedicated Section</span><span>GENERALLY not permitted</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Fresh-start reorganization accounting</span><span>Permitted, dedicated Section</span><span>Narrow, indirect equivalents only</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ASPE'S EXPLICIT accommodation OF both SCENARIOS is MORE closely ALIGNED with US GAAP'S OWN historical push-DOWN accounting OPTION (ASC 805-50) THAN with IFRS'S more RESTRICTIVE stance — a GENUINE illustration of ASPE'S own ROOTS in North-AMERICAN accounting TRADITION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every comprehensive revaluation event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Comprehensive revaluation adjustment</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge sre">SRE</span></span><span>Typically recognized within retained earnings, not net income</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1582</span><span>Business Combinations — supplies the fair value measurement principles this Section's revaluation exercise applies</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Push-down accounting</div><div class="glossary-def">Reflecting an acquirer's new cost basis directly within the acquired entity's own separate financial statements, following a purchase of substantially all of its equity interests.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Fresh start accounting</div><div class="glossary-def">A comprehensive revaluation of assets and liabilities to fair value upon emergence from a qualifying formal financial reorganization.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
